orthant 1 release

= Out.println(privateKey.uncloak(message.decrypt.as[Text]))
= ```
=
+ The padding which a plain RSA key encrypts with is that of PKCS#1 v1.5. It is provided for the
+ protocols which still use it, and should not be chosen for anything new. `RsaOaep` is the
+ modern padding. Its second type parameter is the hash it uses:
+
+ ```scala
+ val oaepKey = PrivateKey.generate[RsaOaep[2048, Sha2[256]]]()
+
+ val wrappedMessage = oaepKey.public.uncloak:
+   t"secret".encrypt(InitializationVector.random)
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(oaepKey.uncloak(wrappedMessage.decrypt.as[Text]))
+ ```
+
+ An `Rsa` key and an `RsaOaep` key are the same document. The type is what selects the
+ padding, and keeps one key from being used under both.
+

= Out.println(signer.public.verify(t"a forged document", signature).inspect)
= ```
=
+ RSA has a second signature scheme as well. `RsaPss` signs with the randomized padding of
+ RSASSA-PSS, which is what TLS 1.3 and the `PS256` algorithm of [JOSE](jose.md) require:
+
+ ```scala
+ val pssSigner = PrivateKey.generate[RsaPss[2048]]()
+ val pssSignature = pssSigner.sign(document)
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(pssSigner.public.verify(document, pssSignature).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ An ECDSA signature is a pair of integers, which a provider writes as DER. JOSE and COSE
+ write the same pair as two fixed-width numbers instead. `Ecdsa.concatenation` and
+ `Ecdsa.signature` convert between the two forms:
+
+ ```scala
+ val fixedWidth: Optional[Data] = Ecdsa.concatenation(signature)
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(fixedWidth.let(_.length).or(0).show)
+ ```
+

= sessionKey.unseal(nonce, t"another header".bytestream, box).read[Text]
= ```
=
+ ### Deriving and wrapping keys
+
+ The secret which a key agreement produces is not itself a key, and neither is a password. A
+ *key-derivation function* turns either into key material of the length a cipher needs.
+ `KeyDerivation` provides three, each over a hash which is named as a type:
+
+ ```tabular
+ column  Function
+ column  Standard
+ column  For
+ row  `hkdf`  RFC 5869  a key from an agreed secret, as TLS 1.3 and most modern protocols derive theirs
+ row  `concat`  NIST SP 800-56A  the same, as the `ECDH-ES` algorithms of [JOSE](jose.md) do it
+ row  `pbkdf2`  RFC 8018  a key from a password, deliberately slowly
+ ```
+
+ ```scala
+ val material: Data = t"an agreed secret".in[Data]
+ val derived: Data = KeyDerivation.hkdf[Sha2[256]](material, Data(), t"session".in[Data], 32)
+ val slow: Data = KeyDerivation.pbkdf2[Sha2[256]](t"hunter2".in[Data], t"salt".in[Data], 4096, 32)
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(derived.length.show)
+ Out.println(slow.serialize[Hex])
+ ```
+
+ *Key wrapping* is encryption whose plaintext is another key. `AesKeyWrap` is the algorithm
+ of RFC 3394. It needs no nonce, and its integrity check is built in, so unwrapping with the
+ wrong key raises a `Crypto.Error`:
+
+ ```scala
+ val wrappingKey = SymmetricKey.generate[AesKeyWrap[256]]()
+ val wrapped: Data = wrappingKey.wrap(derived)
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(wrapped.length.show)
+ Out.println(wrappingKey.unwrap(wrapped).length.show)
+ ```
+
+ `AesCbcHmac` is a second authenticated cipher, beside AES-GCM. It is AES in CBC mode with an
+ HMAC over the result, as JOSE's `A128CBC-HS256` defines it, and it seals and opens in the same
+ way. Its size is that of the whole key, half of which is for each part.
+

= [HTTP server](http-server.md) presents such a certificate over TLS.
=
+ A certificate's fields are read back from it. Its key is read as a key of a named cipher,
+ which the certificate's own algorithm must agree with:
+
+ ```scala
+ val named: Optional[Text] = certificate.subject.let(_.commonName)
+ val certified: PublicKey[Rsa[2048]] = certificate.key[Rsa[2048]]
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(named.or(t"nobody"))
+ Out.println(certificate.authority.inspect)
+ ```
+
+ `serial`, `issuer`, `notBefore`, `notAfter` and `extension` read the other fields, and
+ `authority` says whether a certificate is a certificate authority's.
+
+ `Certificate.validate` checks a *chain* of certificates against a set of trusted roots. The
+ chain begins with the certificate which is being relied on, and each certificate is followed
+ by that of the authority which issued it. Every certificate must be within its validity
+ period, by the `Clock` in scope, and must be signed by the next. Every issuer must be an
+ authority, and the last certificate must be a trusted root, or be issued by one:
+
+ ```scala
+ val authorityKey = PrivateKey.generate[Ecdsa[256]]()
+ val authorityName = Distinguished(commonName = t"Example Root")
+
+ val trusted =
+   Certificate.selfSigned(authorityName, authorityKey, validity, BigInt(1), authority = true)
+
+ val issued =
+   Certificate.issued(subject, tlsKey.public, authorityName, authorityKey, validity, BigInt(2))
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Certificate.validate(List(issued), List(trusted))
+ Out.println(issued.signedBy(trusted).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ A chain which does not lead to a trusted root raises a `Certificate.Path.Error`. Revocation
+ and name constraints are not examined, so this suits a closed set of roots which were chosen
+ for one purpose, and not the roots of the public web.
+
+ ### COSE
+
+ [COSE](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9052) is the counterpart of [JOSE](jose.md) for
+ CBOR. `Cose` signs a payload with a private key, or authenticates it with a symmetric key, and
+ `authenticate` checks the result. The key's type chooses the algorithm, and a message whose
+ header names another is refused:
+
+ ```scala
+ val envelope: Data = Cose(document.in[Data], signer).in[Data]
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(envelope.authenticate[Cose](signer.public).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ A `Cose.Key` is a public key in COSE's own form, which is how a [WebAuthn](webauthn.md)
+ authenticator describes the key of a new credential. It records the algorithm the key is to
+ be used with, and reads back as a key of a named cipher:
+
+ ```scala
+ val coseKey: Cose.Key = Cose.Key(signer.public)
+ val restoredKey: PublicKey[Ecdsa[256]] = coseKey.key[Ecdsa[256]]
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ Out.println(coseKey.algorithm.show)
+ ```
+

